---
# Staff login — the way in to the production content manager.
#
# OUTSIDE the protected /admin/ path on purpose. A visitor who is not signed in
# has to land somewhere that will actually load, and a page that is itself
# behind the role check would give them Netlify's raw 401 instead of a sentence
# they can act on. Invitation and recovery links land here too, and the person
# following one has no role yet.
#
# The sign-in form is ours; @netlify/identity owns the credentials, the tokens
# and the cookies. It is a headless library with no UI of its own — see
# src/admin/staff-login.js.
#
# Deliberately NOT using the shared site layout: this must not pull in the
# public navigation or js/main.js, and it must not appear in any listing.
permalink: /staff-login/index.html
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <!-- An operational route, not content. Never indexed, and excluded from the
         sitemap by src/_data/publicRoutes.js. -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow, noarchive" />
    <title>Staff login — Federation of Polish Student Societies in the UK</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="{{ site.logo }}" />
    <style>
{{ cmsConfig.staffLoginStyles | safe }}
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main class="login">
      <img class="login-logo" src="{{ site.logo }}" alt="" />
      <h1>Federation of Polish Student Societies in the UK</h1>
      <p class="login-lead" id="login-lead">Staff login</p>

      <p class="login-note">
        Sign in with the account you were invited with. Accounts are created by
        the Federation — there is no public sign-up.
      </p>

      <p class="login-message login-notice" id="login-notice" role="status" hidden></p>
      <p class="login-message login-problem" id="login-problem" role="alert" hidden></p>

      {#-
        Hidden until the script decides what this visit is: an ordinary sign-in,
        an invitation, or a password reset. Showing an e-mail box to somebody
        following an invitation link would be asking the wrong question.
      -#}
      <form class="login-form" id="login-form" hidden>
        <div class="login-field">
          <label for="login-email">E-mail address</label>
          <input type="email" id="login-email" name="email" autocomplete="username"
                 required autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false" />
        </div>
        <div class="login-field">
          <label for="login-password">Password</label>
          <input type="password" id="login-password" name="password"
                 autocomplete="current-password" required />
        </div>
        <button type="submit" class="login-button" id="login-submit"
                data-label="Sign in">Sign in</button>
      </form>

      {#-
        External sign-in providers.

        DISCOVERED, NOT HARD-CODED. The script asks Netlify Identity which
        providers this site actually has enabled (getSettings) and renders a
        button for each. A button for a provider nobody configured would take a
        person to an error page, and a provider enabled later would need a code
        change to appear.

        Whichever provider is used, access still depends on an invited account
        carrying a role: signing in proves who somebody is, not that they were
        invited.
      -#}
      <div class="login-providers" id="login-providers" hidden></div>

      {#-
        Shown when somebody arrives here from the CMS rather than to sign in:
        after logging out, after a session expired, or because the account has
        no role. They asked to leave, or were sent away — offering the way back
        to the public site is more use than a form they may not want.
      -#}
      <p class="login-help login-exit" id="login-exit" hidden>
        <a href="/">Back to polsocfederation.pl</a>
      </p>

      <p class="login-help">
        <button type="button" class="login-link" id="login-forgot">Forgot password</button>
      </p>

      <p class="login-help">
        Trouble signing in? Ask the Federation President or the person who
        invited you. <a href="/">Back to the website</a>
      </p>
    </main>

    <!--
      @netlify/identity, bundled from the pinned package by
      scripts/build-identity.js and served from this origin.

      Not a CDN script and not the legacy netlify-identity-widget: the version
      is fixed by package-lock rather than by whatever a third party serves
      today, and the page's Content-Security-Policy needs no foreign script
      origin at all.
    -->
    <script src="/staff-login/netlify-identity.js"></script>
    <script>
{{ cmsConfig.staffLoginScript | safe }}
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
